# THE OUTPUT GUARD, and what `heroes fmt` makes of one source (design.md
# §4.15, §3.3). Moved here from `cli/syntax_cmds.hero` on 2026-09-28 for
# panel 179's probe, which asks the same question of every variant it makes
# and must ask it the way `fmt` does: `checked` is the one function both
# call, so the probe cannot judge a formatter `fmt` is not.
#
# `fmt` checked that its INPUT parsed and never looked at what it wrote,
# so it deleted seven arms out of a compiler source file at exit 0 (defect
# 003, the third instance of this class after panels
# 014 and 060/061, every one found by a person).
#
# FOUR CHECKS OF THE OUTPUT, and they are four different properties,
# beside the printer's own word that it kept every comment where it was
# written (defect 101). The output must PARSE, because a formatter that
# emits source the parser refuses has produced a non-program. Formatting it
# again must give the same TEXT, because a canonical form that is not a
# fixpoint is not canonical. And it must hold the same TREE, which is the
# one the other two cannot do: output that parses and is a fixpoint can
# still mean something else. Panel 061's deleted `partial`, panel 060's
# hoisted `record`, and a record-field remark promoted to documentation are
# all of that kind. And every comment must sit where it sat among the code,
# which the tree cannot say since it keeps no comment but a doc: defect
# 101's comments moved off the line they trailed and out of their brackets
# at exit 0, and the one moved off a joined signature's `(` line was the
# printer's own blind spot, so this check asks the two files rather than
# any printer: each comment's block, by the rule the printer places it
# with, and the tokens `fmt` keeps on either side of it
# (`print/anchors.hero`, which says what it cannot see).
#
# The third check is `assert_canonical`, `dump(text) == dump(fmt(text))`,
# which the Rust bootstrap had, the port never brought over, and `dump.hero`
# went on claiming as live for a week.
#
# HOW THE TWO TREES ARE MADE COMPARABLE, because they are not by default:
# a source may hold the whole module graph plus the library appended, while
# the output is one file. So the root file's own text is parsed standalone
# and compared against the output parsed standalone, one file against one
# file, both named `path` so `dump_ast`'s first line agrees. Two things
# make the dump invariant under legitimate formatting, both measured rather
# than assumed: `bodies.hero` runs an integer literal through
# `canonical_int`, so `fmt`'s one literal rewrite (`0xFF` to `0xff`) dumps
# identically; and the dump carries names, structure and doc comments,
# never spans or columns.
#
# ONE STEP BEFORE THE PRINTER, judged on its own (panel 238). `fmt` writes a
# file's `use` lines as one block in one order, and the printer keeps source
# order, so the reorder is a pass over the text (`print/use_order.hero`) that
# `checked` runs first. The four checks pair the printer's input with its
# output, and a moved line is no pair, so the pass has a judge of its own,
# `reorder_refused`: the lines it wrote are the lines it read, a blank one
# aside, each as many times, and the tree is the same with its `use` lines
# read as a set, every declaration and its doc as it was. The printer then
# reads the ordered text and the four checks judge it against that text; the
# second pass asks the reorder to leave the output unchanged as well.
#
# Every failure is the COMPILER's (§7): `fmt` exits 2, the author's file
# untouched.

use bytes
use diag
use parse
use print/anchors
use print/dump
use print/fmt
use print/owners
use print/page
use print/use_order
use source
use source_extent
use text_lines

# What `fmt` made of one source: the input's parse, and when it holds no
# diagnostic, the canonical text, the parse of that text, and the guard's
# word on it: `ok` with the reason when `fmt` accuses itself, `fail` when
# every check held. With diagnostics nothing is formatted, `formatted` is
# empty and `output` is the parse of that empty text.
#
# `ordered` is the text the printer read, the input with its `use` block in
# the one order (`print/use_order.hero`), and `ordered_input` its parse: the
# input itself and its parse where the block already was. Where the reorder's
# own judge refuses it, nothing is printed: `formatted` is empty and `output`
# the parse of the ordered text it refused.
record Checked
    input: parse.ParseOutput
    ordered: str
    ordered_input: parse.ParseOutput
    formatted: str
    output: parse.ParseOutput
    refusal: str?

# The formatter and its guard over `s`, read as `fmt` reads a file: the
# root file alone (`input.read_root_only`), named `path`.
function checked(path: str, s: source.Source) -> Checked
    first = parse.parse_source(s)

    if first.diagnostics.len() > 0
        return Checked(
            input: first,
            ordered: "",
            ordered_input: first,
            formatted: "",
            output: parse.parse(""),
            refusal: fail(code: "unformatted", msg: "the file has diagnostics")
        )

    # The `use` block in its one order, judged before the printer reads it;
    # the printer then reads that text, in source order as ever.
    text = source_extent.user_text(s)
    order = use_order.reordered(s, first.uses, first.comments, first.decls)
    out: parse.ParseOutput @= first
    read_from: source.Source @= s

    if order != text
        read_from @ source.new_source(name: path, text: order)
        out @ parse.parse_source(read_from)
        wrong = reorder_refused(path: path, text: text, order: order, order_parse: out)

        if !wrong.is_err()
            return Checked(input: first, ordered: order, ordered_input: out, formatted: "", output: out, refusal: wrong)
    printed = fmt.format_lexed(out.tree, read_from, out.comments, out.uses, out.decls, out.tokens)
    formatted = page.whole(printed)

    # The file alone is what the guard compares against, and `fmt` read it
    # alone, so this parse and the lines the printer read it with are the
    # guard's own and are handed over rather than made again (defect 105).
    # A source that holds more than the file is parsed alone, as the guard
    # would.
    before: parse.ParseOutput @= out
    read: owners.Lines @= printed.lines

    if read_from.files.len() != 1
        before @ parse.parse(source_extent.user_text(read_from))
        read @ owners.none()
    judged = judged_output(
        path: path,
        original: source_extent.user_text(read_from),
        before: before,
        read: read,
        formatted: formatted
    )
    refusal: str? @= judged.refusal

    # The printer's own check, and the one no other can make: a comment no
    # walk could keep where it was written is printed somewhere else, which
    # changes neither the tree nor the second pass (defect 101).
    if printed.moved > 0
        refusal @ ok(
            "`fmt` cannot keep the comment on line " + printed.moved.to_str() + " of `" + path + "` where it is written"
        )
    return Checked(
        input: first,
        ordered: source_extent.user_text(read_from),
        ordered_input: out,
        formatted: formatted,
        output: judged.output,
        refusal: refusal
    )

# The reorder's own judge, asked before the printer reads its text: `ok`
# with the reason where the pass broke the file, `fail` where it held. The
# lines written are the lines read, a blank one aside, each as many times; and
# the tree is the same, its `use` rows read as a set and every declaration,
# its documentation included, as it was, so a comment the pass made a
# declaration's doc is refused here.
function reorder_refused(path: str, text: str, order: str, order_parse: parse.ParseOutput) -> str?
    if order_parse.diagnostics.len() > 0
        return ok("`fmt` put the `use` lines of `" + path + "` in an order that does not parse")

    if kept(text) != kept(order)
        return ok("`fmt` lost or added a line putting the `use` lines of `" + path + "` in order")
    was = parse.parse(text)
    a = dump.dump_ast(was.tree, source.new_source(name: path, text: text), was.uses, was.decls)
    b = dump.dump_ast(order_parse.tree, source.new_source(name: path, text: order), order_parse.uses, order_parse.decls)

    if settled(a) != settled(b)
        return ok("`fmt` changed the TREE of `" + path + "` putting its `use` lines in order")
    return fail(code: "clean", msg: "the same lines and the same program, its `use` lines in order")

# The text's lines but the blank ones, sorted by their bytes.
function kept(text: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for line in use_order.split_lines(text, starts: text_lines.starts_of(text))
        if !use_order.is_blank(line)
            out @ out.push(line)

    return sort(out)

# A dump with its `use` rows read as a set: those sorted, then every other row
# in its order.
function settled(dumped: str) -> [str]
    uses: [str] @= []
    rows: [str] @= []

    for row in use_order.split_lines(dumped, starts: text_lines.starts_of(dumped))
        if bytes.bytes_at(text: row, at: 0, needle: "  use ")
            uses @ uses.push(row)
        else
            rows @ rows.push(row)

    out: [str] @= sort(uses)

    for row in rows
        out @ out.push(row)

    return out

# The guard over a pair, the original parsed here.
function refuse_output_the_formatter_broke(path: str, original: str, formatted: str) -> str?
    return refused_output(
        path: path,
        original: original,
        before: parse.parse(original),
        read: owners.none(),
        formatted: formatted
    )

# The guard, handed the original's parse, standalone, and the lines the
# first pass read it with, or `owners.none()` (`anchors.first_moved_read`).
function refused_output(path: str, original: str, before: parse.ParseOutput, read: owners.Lines, formatted: str) -> str?
    return judged_output(path: path, original: original, before: before, read: read, formatted: formatted).refusal

# The guard's word and the output's parse, which a second judge reads.
record Judged
    refusal: str?
    output: parse.ParseOutput

function judged_output(path: str, original: str, before: parse.ParseOutput, read: owners.Lines, formatted: str) -> Judged
    after = source.new_source(name: path, text: formatted)
    reparsed = parse.parse(formatted)

    if reparsed.diagnostics.len() > 0
        return Judged(
            refusal: ok("`fmt` produced source that does not parse: " + diag.render_line(reparsed.diagnostics[0], after)),
            output: reparsed
        )
    again = fmt.format_lexed(reparsed.tree, after, reparsed.comments, reparsed.uses, reparsed.decls, reparsed.tokens)

    # The second pass is the whole of `fmt`'s: the reorder must leave the
    # output as it is, as the printer must.
    if page.whole(again) != formatted || use_order.reordered(after, reparsed.uses, reparsed.comments, reparsed.decls) != formatted
        return Judged(refusal: ok("`fmt` is not a fixpoint on its own output for `" + path + "`"), output: reparsed)

    if again.moved > 0
        return Judged(
            refusal: ok("`fmt` cannot keep the comment on line " + again.moved.to_str() + " of its own output for `" + path + "`"),
            output: reparsed
        )

    if before.diagnostics.len() > 0
        # The root file alone does not parse, which `checked` has already
        # ruled out for the whole compilation, so there is no tree to
        # compare against and the two checks above stand on their own.
        return Judged(refusal: fail(code: "clean", msg: "the root file alone does not parse"), output: reparsed)
    first = source.new_source(name: path, text: original)
    was = dump.dump_ast(before.tree, first, before.uses, before.decls)
    now = dump.dump_ast(reparsed.tree, after, reparsed.uses, reparsed.decls)

    if was != now
        return Judged(
            refusal: ok("`fmt` changed the TREE of `" + path + "` — the output parses and is a fixpoint, and it is a DIFFERENT PROGRAM"),
            output: reparsed
        )
    moved = anchors.first_moved_read(
        was: first,
        input: before,
        now: after,
        output: reparsed,
        read_input: read,
        read_output: again.lines
    )

    if moved > 0
        return Judged(
            refusal: ok("`fmt` would move the comment on line " + moved.to_str() + " of `" + path + "` away from the code it was written beside"),
            output: reparsed
        )
    return Judged(
        refusal: fail(code: "clean", msg: "the output parses, is a fixpoint, holds the same tree and every comment's place"),
        output: reparsed
    )

## Tests

test "a clean file is formatted and passes its guard, and one with diagnostics is not formatted"
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: "function main()\n    print(2 + (3 * 4))  # kept\n")
    done = checked(path: "t.hero", s: s)
    assert done.input.diagnostics.len() == 0
    assert done.formatted == "function main()\n    print(2 + 3 * 4)  # kept\n"
    assert done.refusal.is_err()
    assert done.output.comments.len() == 1
    broken = checked(path: "t.hero", s: source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: "function main(\n"))
    assert broken.input.diagnostics.len() > 0
    assert broken.formatted == ""
    assert broken.refusal.is_err()

# Defect 503, 2026-10-08: a map opened where its line begins, a constant's
# body or the value line of a block, is printed in the spread form of
# design.md §4.9, its `{` alone on the line over one entry a line, and the
# guard reads that output back as the map it is. The lexer read such a `{` as
# a body's brace, the habit of other languages (defect 504), so `fmt`
# refused its own output at exit 2 as a compiler bug on programs `check`
# accepts: a map broken after a key's `:`, after a value, before its `}`,
# nested, in a match arm's block, written on one line past the width, which
# `fmt` breaks itself, and a map alone as a statement, which `check` refuses
# and the parser does not, so `fmt` owes it its canonical form.
test "a map opened where its line begins is printed spread and passes its guard"
    given = [
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\"ziggy\":\n        42, \"mars\": 7}\n"
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\"ziggy\": 42,\n        \"mars\": 7}\n"
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\"ziggy\": 42, \"mars\": 7, \"a_long_name_here_and_there\": 1, \"another_long_name_here_and_there\": 2, \"and_one_more_name\": 3}\n"
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\"ziggy\": 42, \"mars\": 7\n    }\n"
        "constant NEST: {str: {str: i64}}\n    {\"a\": {\"ziggy\":\n        42}, \"b\": {\"mars\": 7}}\n"
        "function main()\n    {\"ziggy\":\n        42}\n"
        "function f(n: i64) -> {str: i64}\n    return match n\n        1 =>\n            {\"ziggy\":\n                42}\n        _ => {\"mars\": 7}\n"
    ]
    spread = [
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\n        \"ziggy\": 42\n        \"mars\": 7\n    }\n"
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\n        \"ziggy\": 42\n        \"mars\": 7\n    }\n"
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\n        \"ziggy\": 42\n        \"mars\": 7\n        \"a_long_name_here_and_there\": 1\n        \"another_long_name_here_and_there\": 2\n        \"and_one_more_name\": 3\n    }\n"
        "constant AGES: {str: i64}\n    {\n        \"ziggy\": 42\n        \"mars\": 7\n    }\n"
        "constant NEST: {str: {str: i64}}\n    {\n        \"a\": {\"ziggy\": 42}\n        \"b\": {\"mars\": 7}\n    }\n"
        "function main()\n    {\n        \"ziggy\": 42\n    }\n"
        "function f(n: i64) -> {str: i64}\n    return match n\n        1 =>\n            {\n                \"ziggy\": 42\n            }\n        _ => {\"mars\": 7}\n"
    ]

    for at in range(from: 0, to: given.len())
        done = checked(path: "t.hero", s: source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: given[at]))
        assert done.input.diagnostics.len() == 0
        assert done.formatted == spread[at]
        assert done.refusal.is_err()

        # And the printed form is a fixpoint the parser reads as it was written.
        again = checked(path: "t.hero", s: source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: done.formatted))
        assert again.input.diagnostics.len() == 0
        assert again.formatted == done.formatted
        assert again.refusal.is_err()

# Defect 506, 2026-10-08: a comment after the `(` around a map's key stays
# after it. The entry was read as beginning on its key's own line, so the
# comment went above the entry, out of the parentheses, and the guard
# refused at exit 2 (the first two); written on the `{` line, `fmt` moved it
# to trail the `{` and dropped the parentheses, where a list keeps both
# (`[(  # c` over `8` over `)]`), and prints it as the list does now.
test "a comment after the parenthesis around a map's key stays after it"
    given = [
        "function main()\n    m = {\n        (  # c1\n            \"ziggy\"  # c2\n        ): 8\n    }\n"
        "function main()\n    m = {\n        \"mars\": 7\n        (  # c1\n            \"zig\" + \"gy\"\n        ): 8\n    }\n"
        "function main()\n    m = {(  # c1\n            \"ziggy\"\n        ): 8}\n"
    ]
    printed = [
        given[0]
        given[1]
        "function main()\n    m = {\n        (  # c1\n            \"ziggy\"\n        ): 8\n    }\n"
    ]

    for at in range(from: 0, to: given.len())
        done = checked(path: "t.hero", s: source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: given[at]))
        assert done.input.diagnostics.len() == 0
        assert done.formatted == printed[at]
        assert done.refusal.is_err()

# What `reorder_refused` and the fixpoint check read of the dump, written as
# a premise with the test that fires when it dies: a `use` line is a row of
# its own opening `  use `, the alias with it, in the order written. Were the
# dump to print them otherwise, `settled` would read them as ordinary rows and
# every reorder would be refused at exit 2, loud, and this test says why.
test "the dump prints each use line as a row of its own, in the order written"
    text = "use zeta\nuse <core>/x as near_x\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    rows = dump.dump_ast(parsed.tree, source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text), parsed.uses, parsed.decls)
    assert settled(rows) == ["  use <core>/x as near_x", "  use zeta", "file t.hero", ""]
